SSH Key — What is it?

An SSH key is a secure way to prove “this is me” when connecting your computer to a server or GitHub.

Simple idea

Instead of entering your GitHub username and password every time:

Your Computer  🔑 ──────────>  GitHub
              SSH Key

GitHub checks the key and allows access.

====

SSH key has two parts

| Key                | Location        | Purpose                              |
| ------------------ | --------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| **Private Key** 🔒 | Your computer   | Secret identity key — never share it |
| **Public Key** 🔓  | GitHub / Server | Used to verify your identity         |


=======
How they work

YOUR COMPUTER                    GITHUB


Private Key 🔒
      │
      │ Secure authentication
      ▼
                         Public Key 🔓
                              │
                              ▼
                        Access allowed ✅

=======
Why use SSH with GitHub?

Without SSH, when working with GitHub, authentication may be required through other methods. With SSH configured, Git can securely authenticate your computer.

========

For example:

git clone git@github.com:username/project.git

Then:

git add .
git commit -m "Updated project"
git push

=======

Important

Never share your private SSH key.
You only copy your public SSH key and add it to your GitHub account.

In one line

SSH Key = A secure digital key pair that lets your computer authenticate with GitHub or a server. 🔐

An SSH key pair authenticates your computer without a password, and 
adding the public key to GitHub and testing the connection with ssh -T confirms that your Git setup is ready.

